{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 3759 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "989f95a29bda6f11bdc28126c8d6752aeec829f1207955ada54bede9e5b3a3e2" :edb/db-before-t 3758 :edb/db-after-t 3759 :edb/tx-data [[13194139537071 50 #inst "2026-10-18T03:50:03.214Z" 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329750 1071 "Let $V=C[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329750 1071 "Let $V=C[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{2}$\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329750 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{2}$ is true, while statement $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$ is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement “$f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$” is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{2}$ only." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329750 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329800 1071 "Consider the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to this function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329800 1071 "Consider the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to this function?\n- **I.** $[0,1]$\n- **II.** $[1,2]$\n- **III.** $[-1,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329800 1074 "The intermediate value theorem can be applied on closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nThe function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, where $n$ is any integer.\n\n- On the interval $[0,1],$ we **can** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- On the interval $[1,2],$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval contains the point of discontinuity $\\frac{\\pi }{2}≈1.57\\,.$\n\n- On the interval $[-1,0),$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval is not closed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329800 1074 "The intermediate value theorem can be applied on closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nThe function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, where $n$ is any integer.\n\n- On the interval $[0,1],$ we **can** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- On the interval $[1,2],$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval contains the point of discontinuity $\\frac{\\pi }{2}≈1.57\\,.$\n\n- On the interval $[-1,0),$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval is not closed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $[0,1]$ only." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329801 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=x(x+3)(x-2).$ On which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=-5$ has a solution?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329801 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=x(x+3)(x-2).$ On which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=-5$ has a solution?\n- **I.** $[-5,-3]$\n- **II.** $[-3,-2]$\n- **III.** $[-2,0]$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329801 1074 "Since $f(x)$ is a polynomial, it's continuous over the real numbers. Thus, we can apply the intermediate value theorem to any of the given intervals.\n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-70$ | $0$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-5,-3],$ we have $f(-5)=-70$ and $f(-3)=0.$ Since $-5\\in [-70,0],$ the IVT does guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-3,-2],$ we have $f(-3)=0$ and $f(-2)=8.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-2,0],$ we have $f(-2)=8$ and $f(0)=0.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329801 1074 "Since $f(x)$ is a polynomial, it's continuous over the real numbers. Thus, we can apply the intermediate value theorem to any of the given intervals.\n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-70$ | $0$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-5,-3],$ we have $f(-5)=-70$ and $f(-3)=0.$ Since $-5\\in [-70,0],$ the IVT does guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-3,-2],$ we have $f(-3)=0$ and $f(-2)=8.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-2,0],$ we have $f(-2)=8$ and $f(0)=0.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $[-5,-3]$ only." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329803 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}.$ In which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=3$ has a solution? \n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329803 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}.$ In which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=3$ has a solution? \n- **I.** $[-4,-1]$\n- **II.** $[3,5]$\n- **III.** $[-1,4]$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329803 1074 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=2,$ and so the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2$ \n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-4,-1],$ we have $f(-4)=0.5$ and $f(-1)=0.$ Since $3\\notin [0,0.5],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[3,5],$ we have $f(3)=4$ and $f(5)=2.$ Since $3\\in [2,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,4]$ contains the point $x=2.$ Therefore we can not apply the intermediate value theorem on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II  only.\"" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329803 1074 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=2,$ and so the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2$ \n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-4,-1],$ we have $f(-4)=0.5$ and $f(-1)=0.$ Since $3\\notin [0,0.5],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[3,5],$ we have $f(3)=4$ and $f(5)=2.$ Since $3\\in [2,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,4]$ contains the point $x=2.$ Therefore we can not apply the intermediate value theorem on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $[3,5]$ only." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329816 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329816 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The period of the function is $\\pi .$\n\n- **II.** The function is increasing in the interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n\n- **III.** The function has no zeros." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329816 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/77/779d7a31646a229492a8165c8212a5c83ad7d37d843c1c1b3b36766a74bc5124.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The period of the function is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function $y=sec⁡x$ is decreasing on $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has no zeros." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329816 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/77/779d7a31646a229492a8165c8212a5c83ad7d37d843c1c1b3b36766a74bc5124.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The period of the function is $\\pi .$” is false. The period of the function is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement “The function is increasing in the interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$” is false. The function $y=sec⁡x$ is decreasing on $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n\n- Statement “The function has no zeros” is true. The function has no zeros." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329820 1071 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ has no inverse\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=n$\n\nIII. All columns of $A$ are pivot columns\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329820 1071 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329820 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$  \n\nIndeed,  a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$*$\\,\\times$\n\nSince our matrix is not invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, it must be $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.*  $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n$, it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))<n,$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329820 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$  \n\nIndeed,  a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$*$\\,\\times$\n\nSince our matrix is not invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, it must be $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.*  $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n$, it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))<n,$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ has no inverse” only." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329821 1071 "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329821 1071 "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n\n- **II.** $Ax=0$ has no solution\n\n- **III.** The lines $5x-y=-1$ and $-5x+ky=0$ intersect exactly at one point" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329821 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $det(A)\\ne 0$ is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =5k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $5k-5\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “$Ax=0$ has no solution” is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement “The lines $5x-y=-1$ and $-5x+ky=0$ intersect exactly at one point” is true. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statements $det(A)\\ne 0$ and “The lines $5x-y=-1$ and $-5x+ky=0$ intersect exactly at one point” are true." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329821 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =5k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $5k-5\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329822 1071 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the unique solution of $Ax=0,$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$-matrix, is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ .*\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329822 1071 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the unique solution of $Ax=0,$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$-matrix, is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ .*\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $A$ is invertible\n- **III.** ${A}^{-1}$ does not exist\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329822 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options $det(A)\\ne 0$ and “$A$ is invertible” give us true statements:\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $det(A)\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $A$ is invertible .*$\\,✓$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329822 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and II give us true statements:\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $det(A)\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $A$ is invertible .*$\\,✓$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329823 1071 "Given that a function $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1$, is differentiable at $x=2$, and has a vertical asymptote at $x=3$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329823 1071 "Given that a function $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1$, is differentiable at $x=2$, and has a vertical asymptote a