\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329861 1071 "![](images/2f/2f53373ebbb7e812471001388ba88122afbde33cd3317c454466168b3e633b58.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $x=-3$\n- **II.** $x=-2$\n- **III.** $x=0$\n- **IV.** $x=2$\n- **V.** $x=3$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329873 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$ below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3, & x<1 \\\\ 1-{x}^{3}, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329873 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$ below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3, & x<1 \\\\ 1-{x}^{3}, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** It has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n- **II.** It has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$\n- **III.** It's continuous at $x=1.$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329873 1074 "For $x=1,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(1)=1-{1}^{3}=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+3)=({1}^{2}+3)=4\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1-{x}^{3})=(1-{1}^{3})=0.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\nHence, I is true, while II and III are false." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329873 1074 "For $x=1,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(1)=1-{1}^{3}=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+3)=({1}^{2}+3)=4\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1-{x}^{3})=(1-{1}^{3})=0.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\nHence, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$” is true, while “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$” and “It's continuous at $x=1.$” are false." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329878 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x-6,\\, & x<1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329878 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x-6,\\, & x<1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$\n- **II.** $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$\n- **III.** $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329878 1074 "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $g(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $g(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={1}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & =3(1)-6 \\\\  & =3-6 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & ={1}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=1,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329878 1074 "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $g(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $g(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={1}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & =3(1)-6 \\\\  & =3-6 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & ={1}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=1,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$”; “$g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$”; “$g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$”." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329886 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4]?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186329886 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4]?$\n- **I.** $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- **II.** $f(x)={x}^{4}$\n- **III.** $h(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186329886 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- Th