8 1071 "Which of the following is a $3\\times 2$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nL = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nN = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186335979 1071 "Which of the following is a $4\\times 3$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186335979 1071 "Which of the following is a $4\\times 3$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nL = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & \\sqrt{2} & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nN = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186335981 1071 "![](images/86/86aac25c634d29639329ae53209141ce81a3a82a70d42a4c2dfc6d356ee378b8.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCD$ shown above, which of the following are equal to the vector $\\overset{\\to }{DB}?$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BA}-\\overset{\\to }{CA}+\\overset{\\to }{CD}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186335981 1071 "![](images/86/86aac25c634d29639329ae53209141ce81a3a82a70d42a4c2dfc6d356ee378b8.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCD$ shown above, which of the following are equal to the vector $\\overset{\\to }{DB}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186335981 1074 "- For the $1$st expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB} & =(\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC})+\\overset{\\to }{CB} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $2$nd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $3$rd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA}-\\overset{\\to }{CA}+\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =(\\overset{\\to }{BA}-\\overset{\\to }{CA})+\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =(\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC})+\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BC}+\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BD} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{DB}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186335981 1074 "- For the $1$st expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB} & =(\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC})+\\overset{\\to }{CB} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $2$nd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $3$rd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA}-\\overset{\\to }{CA}+\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =(\\overset{\\to }{BA}-\\overset{\\to }{CA})+\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =(\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC})+\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BC}+\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BD} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{DB}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}$; $\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ only." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186336011 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186336011 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(M)=1$\n- **III.** $det(M)=0$\n\n \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186336011 1074 "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-4-12)+2(-2+6)+1(4+4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-16)+2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-16+8+8 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186336011 1074 "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-4-12)+2(-2+6)+1(4+4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-16)+2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-16+8+8 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$M$ is a singular matrix” and $det(M)=0$ are true." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186336016 1071 "Which of the following numbers lie in the second quadrant in an Argand diagram?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186336016 1071 "Which of the following numbers lie in the second quadrant in an Argand diagram?\n- **I.** $-2+\\text{i}$\n- **II.** $-3-2\\text{i}$\n- **III.** $-3+2\\text{i}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186336016 1074 "- The complex number $-2+\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,1)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-3-2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-3$ and the imaginary part $y=-2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-3,-2)$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-3+2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-3$ and the imaginary part $y=2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-3,2)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n![](images/d6/d683b808bee4b7893952adbaf87940066f9ff7d61c99ce7cc7e05fc9a4fbe02f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186336016 1074 "- The complex number $-2+\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,1)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-3-2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-3$ and the imaginary part $y=-2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-3,-2)$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-3+2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-3$ and the imaginary part $y=2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-3,2)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n![](images/d6/d683b808bee4b7893952adbaf87940066f9ff7d61c99ce7cc7e05fc9a4fbe02f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-2+\\text{i}$; $-3+2\\text{i}$ only." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186338747 1071 "Given that $det({A}^{T})=8$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186338747 1071 "Given that $det({A}^{T})=8$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=0$\n- **II.** $2\\text{det}(A)+\\text{det}(I)=16$\n- **III.** $3\\text{det}(I)-2\\text{det}(A)=-13$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186338747 1074 "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)=\\text{det}({A}^{T})=8\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=0$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=8-8=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $2\\text{det}(A)+\\text{det}(I)=16$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n2\\text{det}(A)+\\text{det}(I)=2\\cdot 8+1=17\\ne 16.\n$$\n\n- Statement $3\\text{det}(I)-2\\text{det}(A)=-13$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n3\\text{det}(I)-2\\text{det}(A)=3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 8=-13.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=0$; $3\\text{det}(I)-2\\text{det}(A)=-13$ only." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186338747 1074 "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)=\\text{det}({A}^{T})=8\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=8-8=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n2\\text{det}(A)+\\text{det}(I)=2\\cdot 8+1=17\\ne 16.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n3\\text{det}(I)-2\\text{det}(A)=3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 8=-13.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186338767 1071 "Let $⟨-1,y⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+ky &=-4 \\\\ 3x-4y &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186338767 1071 "Let $⟨-1,y⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+ky &=-4 \\\\ 3x-4y &=5.\\end{a