x=-1$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement “The function is differentiable at $x=1$” is false. From the graph we can see that $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$ So, it can't be differentiable there.\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=2$” is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\nTherefore, only statements “The function is continuous at $x=-1$” and “The function is continuous at $x=2$”  are true." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186341931 1071 "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a corner point at $x=2,$ and is differentiable at $x=4$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186341931 1071 "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a corner point at $x=2,$ and is differentiable at $x=4$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n\n- **II.** It is **not** differentiable at $x=2$\n\n- **III.** It is differentiable at $x=0$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186341931 1074 "- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$ is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n\n- Statement “It is **not** differentiable at $x=2$” is true. The function has a corner point at $x=2$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable at $x=0$” is false.  Continuity does not imply differentiability. For instance, the function  $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous at $x=0$ but it is not differentiable there." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186341931 1074 "- Statement I is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a corner point at $x=2$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is false.  Continuity does not imply differentiability. For instance, the function  $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous at $x=0$ but it is not differentiable there." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186341933 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-1, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 1, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186341933 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-1, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 1, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** It is continuous and differentiable at $x=0$\n- **II.** It is **not** continuous at $x=0$ but it is differentiable at $x=0$\n- **III.** It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-1,1)$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186341933 1074 "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement I is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(0)=1,\n$$\n\n \nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ Therefore, it's not differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186341933 1074 "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement “It is continuous and differentiable at $x=0$” is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(0)=1,\n$$\n\n \nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ Therefore, it's not differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement “It is **not** continuous at $x=0$ but it is differentiable at $x=0$” is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement “It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-1,1)$” is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only statement “It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-1,1)$” is true." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186341934 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x<1 \\\\ 2, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186341934 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x<1 \\\\ 2, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** It is continuous at $x=1$\n- **II.** It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- **III.** It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186341934 1074 "- Statement I and II are true. Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly at  $x=1.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x+