ment “It's neither a type I nor a type II region” is true." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186342014 1071 "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=0$ in the usual way. Given that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186342014 1071 "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=0$ in the usual way. Given that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $A$ is not invertible\n- **III.** The system $Ax=b$ has no solution for some $2\\times 1$ column vectors $b.$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186342014 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement $det(A)\\ne 0$ is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is not invertible” is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The system $Ax=b$ has no solution for some $2\\times 1$ column vectors $b.$” is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement $det(A)\\ne 0$ is true." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186342014 1074 "Recall that given