e unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $det(A)=0$ is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $2k-6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “The lines $2x+2y=2$ and $3x+ky=0$ intersect at infinitely many points” is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- Statement “$Ax=0$ has the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$” is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statement “$Ax=0$ has the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$” is true." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186342017 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $2k-6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- Statement III is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statement III is true." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186342018 1071 "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 8.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186342018 1071 "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 8.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** The system $Ax=b$ has infinitely many solutions\n- **III.** The lines $x+2y=2$ and $4x+ky=0$ don't intersect" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186342018 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $det(A)\\ne 0$ is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k-8\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 8.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n