ular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, the functions $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$, $g(x)=|{x}^{2}+1|$, and $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186345345 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|{x}^{2}+1|$ is equivalent to $p(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ so it is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, the functions I, II, and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186348065 1071 "![](images/3c/3c0dc4b8f7f45a5bc1177778431eccf9bcaac1bf6d520d605f9e6bc08d5b1d91.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348065 1071 "![](images/3c/3c0dc4b8f7f45a5bc1177778431eccf9bcaac1bf6d520d605f9e6bc08d5b1d91.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-2$\n\n- **II.** $x=0$\n\n- **III.** $x=2$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186348065 1074 "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-2,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186348065 1074 "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-2,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=0$; $x=2$ only." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348066 1071 "![](images/89/890e7eaf519a65ca19d4a90b604e7d7a8ab7054635296c4b08f000546f23b545.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348066 1071 "![](images/89/890e7eaf519a65ca19d4a90b604e7d7a8ab7054635296c4b08f000546f23b545.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-2$\n\n- **II.** $x=1$\n\n- **III.** $x=3$" 13194139537071 175