 is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ which has the solution $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186348140 1074 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(2-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ over the interval $[0,\\pi ].$\n\n![](images/41/410e0af2ed20a065c27da5edaf0095dfd22c8a37a92e3d8fbaeece629cefa278.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0$ when $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<\\pi .$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ which has the solution $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$”; “$f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$” only." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348152 1071 "Given the function $f(x)=2+2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348152 1071 "Given the function $f(x)=2+2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x