tors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348204 1071 "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$\n- **II.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=3$\n- **III.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186348204 1074 "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the given span and this basis contains $3$ vectors, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186348204 1074 "Recall that a