nullity}(A)=0,$ then  $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348208 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is **not** invertible.* $\\,\\times$\n\nBy the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ we must have that $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186348208 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is **not** invertible.* $\\,\\times$\n\nBy the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ we must have that $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(A)=n$; $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}$ only." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348211 1071 "For the function $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}{e}^{x},$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348211 1071 "For the function $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}{e}^{x},$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The global maximum value is $\\frac{9}{e}$\n- **II.** The global maximum value is $4$\n- **III.** The global minimum value is $e$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186348211 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}{e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2(x-2){e}^{x}+(x-2)^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(x-2){e}^{x}[2+x-2] \\\\  & =x(x-2){e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nx(x-2){e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\,2.\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1-2)^{2}{e}^{-1}=\\frac{9}{e}≈3.31 \\\\ f(0) & =(0-2)^{2}{e}^{0}=4 \\\\ f(1) & =(1-2)^{2}{e}^{1}=e≈2.72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=4,$ so the global maximum is $4.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=e,$ so the global minimum is $e.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](images/ad/adeb7eb92f270a216f30cd0466e91d0363c5a50b17053316e6e9f44d0dfaaf23.png)" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186348211 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}{e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2(x-2){e}^{x}+(x-2)^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(x-2){e}^{x}[2+x-2] \\\\  & =x(x-2){e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nx(x-2){e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\,2.\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1-2)^{2}{e}^{-1}=\\frac{9}{e}≈3.31 \\\\ f(0) & =(0-2)^{2}{e}^{0}=4 \\\\ f(1) & =(1-2)^{2}{e}^{1}=e≈2.72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=4,$ so the global maximum is $4.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=e,$ so the global minimum is $e.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The global maximum value is $4$”; “The global minimum value is $e$” only.\n\n![](images/ad/adeb7eb92f270a216f30cd0466e91d0363c5a50b17053316e6e9f44d0dfaaf23.png)" 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348213 1071 "For the function $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ defined on the closed interval $[-2,1],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348213 1071 "For the function $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ defined on the closed interval $[-2,1],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The global minimum value is $-1$\n- **II.** The global maximum value is $1$\n- **III.** The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186348213 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1({x}^{2}+1)-x(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pm 1,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(-2)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ f(-1) & =-\\frac{(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=-\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n![](images/f3/f3165b8b46c8ac0ab798ae6162d4b4facb92367f5072598bef936801bce44fc4.png)" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186348213 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1({x}^{2}+1)-x(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pm 1,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(-2)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ f(-1) & =-\\frac{(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=-\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}$” only.\n\n![](images/f3/f3165b8b46c8ac0ab798ae6162d4b4facb92367f5072598bef936801bce44fc4.png)" 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348221 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix which has only $1$ non-pivot column?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348221 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix which has only $1$ non-pivot column?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is  **not** invertible\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186348221 1074 "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{5} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=5=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ has $n=5$ columns and $1$ of them is a non-pivot one, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5-1=4\\ne 5.\n$$\n\n Thus, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is  **not** an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=4$,  then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $n=5$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186348221 1074 "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{5} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=5=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is  **not** invertible” is true. Since $A$ has $n=5$ columns and $1$ of them is a non-pivot one, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5-1=4\\ne 5.\n$$\n\n Thus, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is  **not** an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement “$\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$” is false. Since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=4$,  then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$ is true. Indeed, since $n=5$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is  **not** invertible”; $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$ only." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348222 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix with only  $1$ pivot column?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537071 17592186576411 true] [17592186348222 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix with only  $1$ pivot column?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- **II.** $A$ is not  invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$" 13194139537071 17592186576411 false] [17592186348222 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{4} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=4=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)=3$ is false. Since $A$ has just $1$ pivot column, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=1.$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is not  invertible” is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=4.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$ is true. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 1+\\text{n